+### RBR-12 — Incremental learn again and eligible prefix drift
+
+After an edit, the next Run starts a fresh browser and normally replays unchanged learned steps 1…k−1 model-free, recapturing fresh bindings under their existing server-issued IDs, then learns k…n. Steps after k are learned again even when their text is unchanged. The shipped copy includes `Steps changed — next Run learns from step {k}`, `Repeating saved steps 1–{(k-1)}…`, and `Learning from step {k}`. `Learn again from step {N}` deliberately invalidates that step and suffix; it does not restore a lost live session or retry unknown effects. Login/secret rules apply to prefix replay.
+
+There is a supported earlier-switch exception. Eligible target/observation drift at j < k (`TARGET_DRIFT`, locator ambiguity or stale generation) switches the same session to learning from j under the existing pre-effect/no-effect proof. The current runner supports both pre-admission drift and admitted drift proven before invocation; this is not permission to repeat a dispatched effect. The value-free drift receipt records j; the publication marks j…k−1 re-learned, not reused, and retains the combined replay/new-admission provenance. Those newly learned prefix steps charge the same session model budget. Prefix actions already dispatched are never repeated. A factual assertion `FAIL` or `POLICY_REFUSED` in the prefix stops the run; unknown outcomes never trigger automatic replay or a drift recovery retry.
